1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:01,725
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,945 --> 00:00:03,246
The pipeline works.

3
00:00:03,466 --> 00:00:08,487
Now we finish the project: the
report, the command line, a demo that includes

4
00:00:08,487 --> 00:00:11,096
failures, the tests, and the evaluation.

5
00:00:11,316 --> 00:00:15,445
Then we wrap up the module with a
quiz and your extension challenge.

6
00:00:16,745 --> 00:00:21,329
The Markdown report starts with the
match score and the experience line, then

7
00:00:21,329 --> 00:00:22,936
matched and missing skills.

8
00:00:23,156 --> 00:00:27,386
Every number here was computed
by code, not written by the model.

9
00:00:27,736 --> 00:00:30,211
Suggestions appear
only when there were gaps.

10
00:00:30,431 --> 00:00:35,138
And if the grounding check dropped a
skill, the report says so, rather than hiding

11
00:00:35,138 --> 00:00:35,310
it.

12
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:40,606
The footer lists each prompt with its
fingerprint, the model, the tokens, and the

13
00:00:40,606 --> 00:00:40,912
cost.

14
00:00:41,132 --> 00:00:45,181
Six months from now, you'll know
exactly what produced this report.

15
00:00:45,401 --> 00:00:48,697
A second function
writes the same data as JSON.

16
00:00:49,997 --> 00:00:51,988
The command line has four commands.

17
00:00:52,208 --> 00:00:55,291
Prompts lists versions
and needs no client at all.

18
00:00:55,511 --> 00:00:57,468
Eval runs the evaluation.

19
00:00:57,818 --> 00:01:00,648
Extract prints just the profile, as JSON.

20
00:01:00,868 --> 00:01:03,628
Handy when you're
working on the extraction prompt.

21
00:01:03,978 --> 00:01:09,898
And analyze writes the full report, in
Markdown or JSON, to the screen or to a file.

22
00:01:11,198 --> 00:01:14,992
JSON output, saved to a
file, for other programs to use.

23
00:01:15,342 --> 00:01:18,680
The prompt option runs
the older extraction prompt.

24
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:23,277
The footer shows extract profile at
version one, with its own fingerprint.

25
00:01:23,627 --> 00:01:26,797
And here's an honest
limitation of the offline fake.

26
00:01:27,017 --> 00:01:32,744
Leo's resume says, no experience with
Kubernetes, but the keyword rules list

27
00:01:32,744 --> 00:01:34,402
Kubernetes as a skill.

28
00:01:34,622 --> 00:01:37,845
A model following the version
two prompt should leave it out.

29
00:01:38,065 --> 00:01:41,923
Only the eval with a real
key can tell you whether it does.

30
00:01:43,223 --> 00:01:44,601
Failures on purpose.

31
00:01:44,821 --> 00:01:50,461
A rate limit and then a server error:
the SDK retries twice, and the extraction

32
00:01:50,461 --> 00:01:51,532
still succeeds.

33
00:01:51,882 --> 00:01:56,614
A refusal becomes one clear line,
naming the prompt that was declined.

34
00:01:56,964 --> 00:02:00,848
And a missing file is a clean
error too, with exit code one.

35
00:02:02,148 --> 00:02:05,807
Because the task is split into
steps, each step can be tested.

36
00:02:06,027 --> 00:02:10,879
This test checks the comparison, the
score, and which prompt versions were used.

37
00:02:11,229 --> 00:02:17,237
This one inspects the request the SDK
actually sent: structured output, low effort,

38
00:02:17,237 --> 00:02:21,291
the resume inside its tags,
and the right system prompt.

39
00:02:21,641 --> 00:02:26,547
This proves that code skips the
advice call when nothing is missing: only two

40
00:02:26,547 --> 00:02:27,120
requests.

41
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:33,709
And every failure path has a test:
invented skills, invalid values, cut-off replies,

42
00:02:33,709 --> 00:02:35,863
refusals, and a rejected key.

43
00:02:37,163 --> 00:02:40,992
Twenty-seven tests, all
passing, with no key and no network.

44
00:02:41,212 --> 00:02:44,714
They include the prompt
lock and the evaluation itself.

45
00:02:45,064 --> 00:02:47,484
And mypy is happy with every type hint.

46
00:02:48,784 --> 00:02:53,949
If replies don't fit the schema, read
the field named in the error, and describe

47
00:02:53,949 --> 00:02:58,403
that field more clearly, in the
prompt or in the field's description.

48
00:02:58,753 --> 00:03:02,588
Not complete JSON means
the reply hit max tokens.

49
00:03:02,808 --> 00:03:04,839
Raise the limit, or ask for less.

50
00:03:05,189 --> 00:03:08,559
If the lock test fails,
you edited a released prompt.

51
00:03:08,779 --> 00:03:11,295
Restore it, and add a new version instead.

52
00:03:11,645 --> 00:03:14,809
And if every version
scores the same, you're offline.

53
00:03:15,029 --> 00:03:16,144
That's expected.

54
00:03:16,364 --> 00:03:18,288
Run the evaluation with a key.

55
00:03:19,588 --> 00:03:25,245
Good prompts give context, a clear task,
data in tags, and constraints with reasons.

56
00:03:25,595 --> 00:03:30,098
Use a few varied examples, plain
language, and no outdated tricks.

57
00:03:30,448 --> 00:03:34,398
Give the model the judgment,
and code everything deterministic.

58
00:03:34,748 --> 00:03:38,445
Structured outputs guarantee
shape, and code checks meaning.

59
00:03:38,795 --> 00:03:43,335
And version every prompt, and
measure every change with an evaluation.

60
00:03:44,635 --> 00:03:46,464
Module quiz, question one.

61
00:03:46,684 --> 00:03:51,494
What's the main problem with critical,
you must never, in a prompt for a current

62
00:03:51,494 --> 00:03:51,855
model?

63
00:03:57,555 --> 00:04:02,774
Current models follow instructions
closely, so shouting makes them over-apply the

64
00:04:02,774 --> 00:04:03,096
rule.

65
00:04:03,316 --> 00:04:05,391
Say it once, plainly, with the reason.

66
00:04:06,691 --> 00:04:07,726
Question two.

67
00:04:07,946 --> 00:04:10,365
Why wrap a resume in resume tags?

68
00:04:16,065 --> 00:04:20,386
Tags mark exactly where the data
is, apart from your instructions.

69
00:04:21,686 --> 00:04:22,748
Question three.

70
00:04:22,968 --> 00:04:27,908
What happens to a Pydantic
constraint like greater than or equal to zero?

71
00:04:33,608 --> 00:04:39,648
The SDK validates it when the reply
arrives, and raises a validation error if it

72
00:04:39,648 --> 00:04:40,101
fails.

73
00:04:41,401 --> 00:04:42,464
Question four.

74
00:04:42,684 --> 00:04:47,072
Two prompt versions both score
twenty-one out of twenty-two offline.

75
00:04:47,292 --> 00:04:48,586
What can you conclude?

76
00:04:54,286 --> 00:04:55,680
Nothing about the prompts.

77
00:04:55,900 --> 00:04:58,014
The offline fake ignores wording.

78
00:04:58,234 --> 00:05:03,137
Offline runs test the pipeline,
and only a real model compares prompts.

79
00:05:04,437 --> 00:05:05,700
And question five.

80
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:08,816
Which job belongs to
code, not to the model?

81
00:05:14,516 --> 00:05:16,387
Calculating the match percentage.

82
00:05:16,607 --> 00:05:20,640
Arithmetic belongs in
code: exact, free, and testable.

83
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:23,446
Your extension challenge.

84
00:05:23,666 --> 00:05:27,899
Pick one or more, and write a
test for each, using the fake API.

85
00:05:28,249 --> 00:05:33,762
Write version three of the extraction
prompt, add two hard evaluation cases, and

86
00:05:33,762 --> 00:05:36,311
compare the versions with a real key.

87
00:05:36,661 --> 00:05:41,330
Add a minimum score option that
exits with code two below a threshold.

88
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:46,682
Grade the advice with a second model
call and a short rubric: your first model

89
00:05:46,682 --> 00:05:47,131
grader.

90
00:05:47,481 --> 00:05:51,411
Or accept several job
postings, and rank them by match score.

91
00:05:52,711 --> 00:05:56,534
These are the official
documentation pages for this module.

92
00:05:56,754 --> 00:06:01,912
Start with the prompting best practices:
it's kept up to date for the latest models.

93
00:06:02,132 --> 00:06:05,520
Links are in the description,
along with the full source code.

94
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:11,456
That's Module 3 complete.

95
00:06:11,676 --> 00:06:17,198
You can now write prompts that work
consistently, get validated data back, and prove

96
00:06:17,198 --> 00:06:18,973
a change is an improvement.

97
00:06:19,193 --> 00:06:24,982
In Module 4, we finally build an agent:
the loop at the centre of this course, from

98
00:06:24,982 --> 00:06:28,748
scratch, with a smart
calculator agent as the project.

99
00:06:28,968 --> 00:06:29,832
See you there.
